# Local environment template.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# then fill in the values below. `.env` and every other `.env.*` variant are
# gitignored; this file is the only one that is tracked, and it must never
# carry a real value.
#
# This file holds TWO kinds of variable, and the difference is the whole point.
#
# BUILD-TIME VALUES are read by Astro while the site compiles. The site is
# static — there is no server runtime to read environment variables in — so
# these are baked into the generated HTML. None of them can be a secret, and
# the schema in astro.config.ts marks them `access: 'public'` to say so out
# loud. CLOSEBOT_SOURCE is one.
#
# OPERATOR CREDENTIALS are never read by the build, never appear in the schema,
# and never reach a generated byte. They exist for commands a human or an agent
# runs by hand against an API. They ARE secrets. Their absence from
# astro.config.ts is what keeps them out of the bundle — do not add them there
# to "be consistent", because `context: 'client'` would publish them to every
# visitor. CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN is one.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLOSEBOT_SOURCE — the chat widget's Sales Pixel id
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHERE TO GET IT, in CloseBot:
#
#   Source Settings  →  Setup  →  Sales Pixel
#
# Copy the id from there. It is the value interpolated into the widget's script
# URL as `?source=...` — hence the variable name, which matches the query
# parameter rather than CloseBot's UI label for it.
#
# NOT A SECRET. It ends up in a <script src> that every visitor receives, so
# `view-source:` on any deployed page shows it. Keeping it out of the
# repository is about FORK HYGIENE, not secrecy: without this, anyone who
# clones the repo and deploys it loads *this* account's agent, and a stranger's
# visitors — plus the usage they generate — land in someone else's CloseBot.
#
# LEAVE IT EMPTY AND NO WIDGET RENDERS. That is the correct default for a fork
# or a contributor, and the test suite adapts to it instead of failing: the
# script-count assertion derives its expected number from whether the widget is
# present. Everything else — the site, the budgets, the SEO suite — works
# without it.
#
# WHERE IT IS SET FOR REAL (all three, or that surface loses the widget):
#
#   Cloudflare  →  Workers & Pages → brycedecora → Settings → Builds
#                  → Variables and secrets, as type "Variable"
#                  Set it under BOTH tabs: "Production" and "Previews Base".
#                  They are separate lists; filling in only one leaves PR
#                  preview deploys with no widget.
#
#   GitHub      →  Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions → Variables
#                  (a *variable*, not a secret — it is public by construction)
#                  Note: GitHub does not pass variables to workflows triggered
#                  by a PR from a fork, so an outside contributor's CI builds
#                  without the widget. That is expected and handled.
#
# A missing value fails QUIETLY — no widget, no build error. After a deploy,
# confirm with:
#
#   curl -s https://brycedecora.com/ | grep -o 'cb.js?source=[^"]*'
#
CLOSEBOT_SOURCE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN / CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID — purge the Cloudflare cache
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# OPERATOR CREDENTIALS, not build values. Nothing in src/ reads them and they
# are deliberately absent from the env schema in astro.config.ts. The token is
# a real secret; the zone id is not, but it lives here because the purge
# command below is useless without both.
#
# WHAT THEY ARE FOR. Workers Builds deploys on push and the edge picks up the
# new assets on its own, so purging is NOT a normal part of deploying. This is
# for the case where a stale copy is pinned at one point of presence and some
# readers are served an old page while you see the new one. That failure is
# invisible from a single machine: your requests only ever reach your nearest
# POP, so "it looks fine to me" proves nothing about anyone else.
#
# HOW TO MAKE THE TOKEN, in Cloudflare:
#
#   My Profile  →  API Tokens  →  Create Token  →  Create Custom Token
#
#     Permissions     Zone  ·  Cache Purge  ·  Purge
#     Zone Resources  Include  ·  Specific zone  ·  <your domain>
#
# Scope it exactly that far and no further. A token that can only purge a cache
# is worth little to whoever steals it. "Zone · Edit" on the same zone would
# let the holder repoint your DNS.
#
# The zone id is on that zone's Overview page in the dashboard, lower right.
#
# USING THEM:
#
#   curl -X POST \
#     "https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4/zones/$CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID/purge_cache" \
#     -H "Authorization: Bearer $CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN" \
#     -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
#     --data '{"purge_everything":true}'
#
# A FORK DOES NOT NEED EITHER ONE. Leave them empty. No part of the build, the
# test suite, or CI touches them — they are only ever used by hand, against
# your own zone.
#
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID=
